{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>You shall have no other Elohim before My face.</b> Since He said 'I am YHWH,' and the distinctive Name is in the language of enduring existence — to say that He, blessed be He, is always in His existence: whether in the past, whether in the present, whether in the future, at all times He was, is, and will be in a single existence — it was necessary to say here 'there shall not be to you,' for these others do not endure in their existence and they change. For we find our Sages of blessed memory (Pesachim 23) use the expression 'יהיה' as indicating 'they shall continue in their existence.' Therefore He said here 'there shall not be to you,' to say that they do not endure in their existence, but rather they change and become 'others': whether from the aspect that as long as the idol ages it loses its form, 'the likeness of an idol is despised in the city' (Psalms 73:20); whether from the aspect that they change from silver to gold and to wood and to stone; whether from the aspect that they are 'others' to their worshippers — they cry out to them and they are not answered. Therefore He said 'there shall not be to you,' and the proof of this is that they are indeed 'other Elohim.' And He said 'upon My face' — we have already said above: to mean specifically when My face is turned toward you, then 'you shall have no other Elohim'; but if sin causes there to be a hiding of the face, then of necessity you will worship other Elohim, as Moses warned in the reproof (Deuteronomy 28:64): 'and you will serve there other Elohim' — whose meaning is: since you pay tribute to priests of idolatry, Scripture accounts it as though you served them." "Another matter: since YHWH appeared to them in many faces — at the sea as a mighty warrior and at Mount Sinai as an elder sitting in a session — perhaps you will say, since I appeared in several faces, that there are many authorities; therefore He said 'upon My face,' in the plural sense, to say that all those faces — they are all one." "Another matter: <b>'My face' is an expression of anger and wrath,</b> that if they should say in the manner they said in the days of Ezekiel, 'We will be like the nations,' and so forth, and wish to cast off from themselves the yoke of the kingdom of heaven, the Holy One, blessed be He, answered them: 'And what rises in your spirit shall not be at all — inasmuch as with a strong hand and an outstretched arm and with poured-out wrath I will reign over you' (Ezekiel 20:32–33). Therefore 'upon My face' was stated: for in My anger and in My wrath I will reign over you, should you seek to cast off the yoke of My kingdom from upon you."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {0 "The Talmudic citation פסחים כג (Pesachim 23) likely refers to the page numbering in the printed Talmud; the precise discussion there involves the verb יהיה in relation to halakhic permanence.", 1 "The word פנים (panim) is inherently plural in Hebrew; the comment depends on this grammatical fact being read as carrying a theological charge."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:21:40.322485942Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}